WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.490
The kids who watched the original Masters
of the Universe cartoons are well into adulthood

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.680
now and some might notice that the show isn't
quite what they remember from back in the

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.180
day.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.720
Here are some of the bizarre, hilarious and
worrying things that only adults will notice

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.820
in He-Man's adventures.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.560
Being so distracted by all the thrilling action
and scary monsters, most kids didn't care

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.360
about the ideology that fueled He-Man and
the Masters of the Universe: capitalism.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.600
But present-day parents who are all too used
to their kids begging for toys they've seen

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:37.200
on TV now recognize that one of the main purposes
of the He-Man cartoon was to move merchandise.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.360
The show kicked off an action figure craze
in the '80s, selling hundreds of millions

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.650
of dollars worth of toys, and every episode
of He-Man and the Masters of the Universe

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.000
served as its own 30-minute ad.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.940
Many episodes would introduce some never-before-seen
character, who just so happened to be now

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.580
available in action figure form at toy stores.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.379
These characters all seemed to possess a unique
ability or feature that, conveniently enough,

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.600
really popped when molded in plastic.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.880
Take Mekaneck or Buzz-Off, for example two
characters who can only possibly have been

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.020
conceived so as to make up a shortfall in
the merch budget.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:14.580
There's no other explanation.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.880
He-Man and the Masters of the Universe was
obviously heavily inspired by other shows

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:20.380
and movies.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.480
After all, a muscle-bound guy in a loincloth
fighting monsters in a strange world?

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.080
That's some straight-up Conan the Barbarian
stuff right there.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:34.220
And that's fine Conan itself borrowed plenty
of sci-fi and fantasy tropes, and He-Man does

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:34.800
the same.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.180
That's just how media works.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.320
It's just that adults will notice He-Man's
cribbing is especially lazy.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.920
"He-Man" itself is an old phrase that means
"manly-man", while other hastily named characters

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.700
include a skeleton named "Skeletor" and a
sorceress named "The Sorceress."

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.660
But at least those are mostly innocuous examples.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.240
Some of the other He-Man characters have names
that, well let's just say they take on a different

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.160
dimension once you're all grown up.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.000
Isn't that right, Fisto?

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.620
“And when Fisto offers you his hand boy, that's a big
offer."

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.420
There might not be too many people on Eternia,
but there are clearly plenty of gyms since

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.160
every single character on He-Man and the Masters
of the Universe is in fantastic shape.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.880
All of the male characters are as ripped as
professional bodybuilders, with enormous muscles

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.000
covering each of them from head to toe.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:36.040
The women, meanwhile, fit right in to the
hugely unrealistic '80s standard of beauty.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.540
With the battle of good versus evil that constantly
plagues Eternia, it's hard to imagine how

00:02:41.549 --> 00:02:46.049
the men have four hours a day to lift weights,
or how the women fit in the pilates, yoga,

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:48.319
and aerobics they must be doing on the regular.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.140
Regardless, the men and women of He-Man and
the Masters of the Universe like to show off

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.600
all that hard work and pretty much everyone
wears incredibly revealing clothing.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.240
Except Orko, of course, but that would be
kinda weird.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.560
According to Masters of the Universe lore,
He-Man is the most powerful individual in

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:07.160
existence.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.700
And he's got plenty of powers and tools that
have helped him reach that status: "the power

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:17.730
of Grayskull," for one, as well as brute physical
strength and the Power Sword, a much-coveted,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.120
Excalibur-like relic.

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:25.379
Despite those gifts, however, He-Man is actually
surprisingly wishy-washy most likely because

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:30.129
the show's non-serialized nature means things
always have to get back to normal by the end

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:30.740
of each episode.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.260
As a result, He-Man never really uses much
of the power afforded to him.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.060
He always lets bad guys get away including
Skeletor, who's pretty much king of the bad

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:40.560
guys.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.779
Since he knows he'll be back to fight Skeletor
another day, He-Man always allows him to flee

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.200
back to his headquarters from which he can
continue his campaign of terror.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.620
The show's hero could easily make life better
for the people of Eternia by ending his war

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.129
and killing Skeletor with the Power Sword
the first chance he gets.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.240
But then there'd be no more show, would there?

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.300
Having dominion over a whole planet or even
the universe is no easy feat, and not even

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.100
an evil, all-powerful sorcerer can do it alone.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.940
Skeletor boasts a huge crew of powerful servants,
all loyally devoted to helping their boss

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.700
destroy He-Man and take over Eternia.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.320
But Skeletor is so evil that he doesn't even
appreciate how baddies like Beast Man and

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.110
Evil-Lyn lay everything on the line for him
day after day, risking life, limb, and more

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.600
in service to this angry, cackling skeleton.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.360
He's forever unhappy with the performance
of his underlings, mainly because they never

00:04:37.371 --> 00:04:42.680
manage to defeat He-Man or complete the task
he's sent them to do and he is always happen

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.230
to let them know it with a threat of violence
of a withering put-down.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.080
"Why do I surround myself with fools?!

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.320
Even the robots smarter than you."

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.460
Skeletor best be careful.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.780
Best case scenario, his put-upon employees
flees to He-Man's side and reveals all kinds

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.180
of secrets to Eternia's hero.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.920
Worst case scenario, they unionize.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.040
And then Skeletor's really in trouble.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.660
Even today, animation is a labor-intensive
and expensive craft.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:16.320
And things were even tougher back in the early
'80s, when He-Man and the Masters of the Universe

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:16.820
aired.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:23.500
Production company Filmation churned out 65
episodes per season, and the crew had to find

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.680
some shortcuts to keep things running smoothly.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.760
Kids with no frame of reference wouldn't notice,
but adults with a cynical eye will easily

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.880
spot the show's cost-cutting measures.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.210
The same handful of music cues are used over
and over, and so are a number of animation

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:38.860
sequences.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:44.260
How many times do we see He-Man and Battle
Cat running or walking, the basic animation

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.540
of their steps repeated for a good chunk of
screentime?

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.920
And of course, at least once per episode,
Prince Adam is going to step in front of Castle

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.540
Grayskull and gradually transform into He-Man.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.120
It's a good lesson for kids: always recycle.

